          %h2#a_little_history_the_beginnings A little history: the beginnings
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+K-theory"} Algebraic K-theory
            grew out of two apparently unrelated areas of algebraic geometry and algebraic
          %p
            topology. The second of these, historically, was the development by Grothendieck of (geometric and topological) K-theory based on projective modules over a ring, or finite dimensional vector bundles on a space, that is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+group"} Grothendieck group
            of the given ring or of the ring of functions on the space. (The connection between these is that the space of global sections of a finite dimensional vector bundle on a nice enough space,
            %math#mathml_16b48915d149d8fa493f01610619540c436146d5_1.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a finitely generated projective module over the ring of continuous real or complex functions on
            %math#mathml_16b48915d149d8fa493f01610619540c436146d5_2.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            \. This latter aspect is where the link with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+K-theory"} topological K-theory
            comes in.)
          %p
            The second input is from of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simple+homotopy+theory"} simple homotopy theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J.+H.+C.+Whitehead"} J. H. C. Whitehead
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reidemeister"} Reidemeister
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                combinatorial homotopy theory
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/combinatorial+homotopy+theory"} ?
          %p
            %em Could one give a ‘combinatorial way of generating all homotopy equivalences, (up to homotopy), starting with some ‘elementary expansions’ and ‘contractions’?
          %p
            He showed the answer was negative, and there was an invariant (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Whitehead+torsion"}> Whitehead torsion
            ) whose vanishing was a necessary and sufficient condition for a homotopy equivalence to be so constructible. That invariant was an element of a group constructed from the stable general linear group over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+ring"} group ring
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+group"} fundamental group
            of the domain space. This was a quotient of what became known as
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_16b48915d149d8fa493f01610619540c436146d5_3.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi K
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℤ
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_1(\mathbb{Z}\pi_1X)
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_16b48915d149d8fa493f01610619540c436146d5_4.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi GL
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℤ
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} GL(\mathbb{Z}\pi_1X)
          %p
            This ties in, even at this basic level with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nPOV"} nPOV
            and the processes around categorification. For a ring,
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_16b48915d149d8fa493f01610619540c436146d5_5.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+group"} Grothendieck group
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_16b48915d149d8fa493f01610619540c436146d5_6.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi K
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_0(R)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/core"} core
            of the category of finitely generated projective modules, and takes its set of connected components, which is just the set of isomorphism classes. This becomes an abelian monoid under direct sum and then a group after group completion. For
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_16b48915d149d8fa493f01610619540c436146d5_7.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi K
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_1(R)
            %math#mathml_16b48915d149d8fa493f01610619540c436146d5_8.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_0
            of that category we get
            %math#mathml_16b48915d149d8fa493f01610619540c436146d5_9.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_1
            of the ring. So
            %math#mathml_16b48915d149d8fa493f01610619540c436146d5_10.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_1
            looks at the loops whilst
            %math#mathml_16b48915d149d8fa493f01610619540c436146d5_11.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_0
            at the connected components.
          %h2#references References
          %p
            One of the best accounts of the history of K-theory is by
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chuck+Weibel"} Chuck Weibel
          %ul
            %li#Weibel99
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Charles+A.+Weibel"}> Charles A. Weibel
              ,
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.rutgers.edu/~weibel/papers-dir/khistory.pdf"} The development of algebraic K-theory before 1980
              published in
              %em Algebra, K-theory, groups, and education:
              %p
                on the occasion of Hyman Bass’s 65th birthday_, a volume of
                %em Contemporary Mathematics, American Mathematical Soc, 1999.
          %p Other historical accounts include
          %ul
            %li#Arlettaz04
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dominique+Arlettaz"}> Dominique Arlettaz
                ,
                %em Algebraic K-theory of rings from a topological viewpoint
                (
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.uiuc.edu/K-theory/0420/Arlettaz-survey.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li#Grayson13
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Grayson"}> Daniel Grayson
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quillen’s work in algebraic K-theory
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.uiuc.edu/~dan/Papers/qs-published-final.pdf"} pdf
        .revisedby
